      "Individual Accounts and Trading Authority",
      "An individual account is opened by one living person who is at least 18 and mentally competent. That person is the only owner. Unless something else is in writing, only the owner can see or direct the account. Without written trading authorization, even a spouse cannot see the account, trade, or take money out.\n\nKey features:\n• Taxes: income and gains go on the owner's tax return.\n• Death: assets pass to the owner's beneficiaries as the owner directed or as the law requires.\n• Trading authorization: the owner can give a third party power to act. There are three kinds:\n  – Limited authority: the person can trade but cannot withdraw money.\n  – Full authority: the person can trade and withdraw money.\n  – Discretionary authority: the adviser or rep can choose what to buy or sell and when. Discretionary power is always limited to trading.\n\nTransfer on death (TOD). A TOD designation lets the owner name beneficiaries and what percent each gets. On a bank account, this is called payable on death (POD), or a Totten trust. The owner keeps full control while alive. At death, the assets go straight to the beneficiaries without probate. A TOD overrides a will, so TOD beneficiaries get the account even if a will says otherwise. A minor cannot be named directly on a TOD or POD account. An adult must be named to manage it until the minor grows up.",
