      "s65-c5-joint",
      "Joint Accounts",
      "A joint account has two or more owners. Each one can trade in it. Every owner signs the account form, which also serves as the joint account agreement, and all owners must agree to close it. Usually any owner can trade and take out money, but checks must be made out to all owners and signed by each. Each owner reports their share of gains and losses on their own tax return.\n\nThe three main types:\n• Joint tenants with rights of survivorship (JTWROS): owners share equally. If one dies, the survivors keep everything.\n• Tenants by the entirety: like JTWROS, but only for married couples. Creditors cannot take the assets unless both spouses owe the debt.\n• Tenants in common (JTIC): each owner's share is usually based on what they put in. When an owner dies, their share goes to their estate.\n\nWith JTWROS and tenants by the entirety, the account passes to the survivor automatically and skips probate, just like TOD accounts. JTIC assets go through probate.\n\nSome states use community property for married couples. Anything bought during the marriage is owned 50/50, with no right of survivorship. When one spouse dies, their half goes to their estate and can pass to any named heir.",
