      "s65-c5-margin",
      "Cash and Margin Accounts",
      "Individual and joint accounts can be cash accounts or margin accounts.\n\nIn a cash account, the customer pays the full price of each purchase. Trades settle one business day after the trade date (T+1). Under SEC rules, a customer gets two more business days to pay if needed.\n\nA margin account lets an investor borrow to buy securities or sell short. The investor pays part of the price, and the broker-dealer lends the rest, up to 50% of the cost. The loan is secured by money or securities in the account. The broker-dealer is the owner of record, and the customer is the beneficial owner.\n\nA new margin customer must sign a margin agreement promptly after the first trade. It has three parts:\n• Hypothecation agreement: lets the firm sell the customer's securities if equity falls below the 25% minimum maintenance level, and lets the firm pledge the securities for its own loans.\n• Credit agreement: sets the terms of the loan, including the interest rate, which is usually tied to the broker call rate.\n• Loan consent agreement: lets the firm lend the customer's margined securities to short sellers. This one is optional.\n\nRetail customers opening margin accounts must get a risk disclosure document, and it must be sent again every year.",
